            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Sagan and the Voyager Missions</strong><br>\nCarl Sagan played a crucial role in the Voyager missions, which sent two spacecraft to study the outer planets. He was responsible for designing the Voyager Golden Record, which contains sounds and images selected to portray the diversity of life on Earth. It was intended to communicate the story of our world to extraterrestrials and remains a milestone in the interstellar message concept.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Cosmos: A Personal Voyage</strong><br>\nIn 1980, Carl Sagan co-wrote and presented \"Cosmos: A Personal Voyage,\" a thirteen-part television series that became one of the most-watched series in public television history. \"Cosmos\" brought the wonders of the universe to millions of viewers and ignited the imagination of the public about the vastness and wonder of space, making science accessible and enjoyable for the common person.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Sagan as an Author</strong><br>\nBesides his work in television, Sagan was an accomplished author, writing books that made complex scientific concepts understandable to the general public. His book \"The Dragons of Eden\" explored the evolution of human intelligence, winning a Pulitzer Prize. \"Contact,\" another notable work, was later adapted into a film, expanding his influence on popular culture.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
